## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to model cross-device costs, trade-offs, and governance for a live feature-flag infrastructure. Tests data locality, privacy, offline behavior, and rollout risk at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Local evaluation cost vs backend traffic
- Data minimization and privacy/compliance
- Rollout strategy and drift management
- Maintenance and rollback costs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CBA calculator (illustrative)
function cba(months, backendCost, localKB, energyPerEval) {
  const backendSavings = backendCost * months;
  const localCosts = localKB * 0.002 + energyPerEval * months;
  return backendSavings - localCosts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle offline devices during experiments?
- What telemetry would you collect to justify cost?

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges practical CBA skills for privacy-conscious features that touch user data, emphasizing cost modeling, data minimization, and risk assessment.

## Key Concepts
- On-device processing, data minimization, and opt-in consent
- Batch sizing, compression, and secure transmission
- Cost levers: storage, CPU energy, network, compliance, and hosting
- Go/no-go criteria tied to break-even and risk tolerance

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchEvents(events, batchSize) {
  const batches = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < events.length; i += batchSize) {
    batches.push(events.slice(i, i + batchSize));
  }
  return batches;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate privacy risk post-implementation (DPIA outcomes, audits)?
- What metrics signal a need to adjust batch size or cadence?

## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests the ability to quantify a data-heavy feature with a clear offline-first constraint, balancing device resources against business value.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architectures
- Local indexing and delta synchronization
- Resource budgeting (CPU, memory, storage)
- Data transfer costs and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```
// Implementation sketch: simple local search index
class LocalIndex {
  constructor() { this.docs = []; }
  build(docs) { this.docs = docs.map(d => ({ id: d.id, text: d.content })); }
  search(q) {
    const terms = q.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
    return this.docs.filter(d => terms.every(t => d.text.toLowerCase().includes(t)));
  }
  deltaUpdate(changes) {
    // apply lightweight patches to docs
    changes.forEach(c => {
      const idx = this.docs.findIndex(d => d.id === c.id);
      if (idx >= 0) this.docs[idx] = { ...this.docs[idx], ...c.patch };
      else this.docs.push({ id: c.id, text: c.patch.content });
    });
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement delta-sync to minimize data transfer while ensuring consistency?
- What privacy controls would you layer to protect sensitive docs during offline usage?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

